 ],
 "literal": "The firebrand at the dry end kindled by the mountain woman / because it is sandalwood, its beautiful smoke, of the nearby / mountain slope, crawls on the flowering branches of the kino tree. / Those who sang of Parampu, having undertaken that very virtue, / Pari also, if supplicants ask, / will not say \"I will not give\", like their mountain.",
 "translation": "The firebrand kindled by the mountain woman is of dry sandalwood; hence, its fragrant, beautiful smoke crawls over the flowering branches of the kino trees on the nearby mountain slopes. If supplicants come begging, Pari, who has made it his virtue to give to those who sing of his Parampu hill, will never say, \"I will not give.\" He gives as generously as his own mountain.",
 "modern": "குறிஞ்சி நிலப் பெண் மூட்டிய காய்ந்த விறகுக் கொள்ளி சந்தன மரத்தால் ஆனது என்பதால், அதன் அழகிய நறுமணப் புகை பக்கத்திலுள்ள மலைச்சரிவில் இருக்கும் வேங்கை மரத்தின் பூத்துக் குலுங்கும் கிளைகளில் தவழ்கிறது. பறம்பு மலையைப் பாடி வரும் இரவலர்கள் கேட்கும்போது, பாரியும் 'தரமாட்டேன்' என்று சொல்லாமல், தன் மலையைப் போன்ற கொடையை அவர்களுக்கு வழங்குவான்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Kapilar praises the extraordinary generosity of his patron, Vēḷ Pāri, the ruler of the Parampu hill. He describes the rich mountain landscape (Kuriñci) where even the firewood used by a mountain woman (kuṟatti) is precious sandalwood (āram), filling the slopes with fragrant smoke that drifts over the flowering kino (vēṅkai) trees. This rich, fragrant environment serves as a metaphor for Pari's boundless wealth and noble nature. When singers and supplicants come to Parampu hill, Pari considers it his sacred duty (aṟam) to give. He never utters a refusal (\"vārēṉ eṉṉāṉ\"), giving away his entire mountain-like wealth to those who ask.",
